data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1791839199831,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Buzz Aldrin each flew on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center on July 16, 1969 at 13:32 UTC, with the command module being the only part that returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The Lunar Module had a descent stage with a large engine and fuel tanks for landing, and an ascent stage carrying a two-astronaut cabin and a small engine.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins stayed in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"command module\",\"service module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong became the first human to walk on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The crew spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"After over 21 hours on the surface, Armstrong and Aldrin rejoined Collins in lunar orbit, and the crew splashed down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Space Race, a Cold War geopolitical competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade's end.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V rocket despite its early spaceflight leads.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Uncrewed Soviet probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21, while Armstrong and Aldrin were still on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, among the most-watched television events in history.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples led to identifying three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years afterwards.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum, while Eagle's descent stage remains on the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"United States\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit Earth on April 12, 1961; Alan Shepard became the first American in space on May 5, 1961 with a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade ends.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the project would be among the most impressive and important for long-range space exploration, and also among the most difficult and expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Earth\",\"Moon\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy says reaching the Moon will be a national effort, since all Americans must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argues space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards threaten everyone equally.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy says conquering space deserves the best of all mankind and offers a chance for peaceful cooperation that may not recur.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answers the question 'why the Moon?' by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Texas\",\"Atlantic\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the US chose to go to the Moon this decade not because it is easy but because it is hard.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener of MIT dubbed it a 'moondoggle'.\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was already named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing project to Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy again proposed a joint Moon expedition at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the joint idea was abandoned after his death.\"},{\"id\":\"7.6\",\"text\":\"An early crucial decision was choosing lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"Moon\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo, which would have three major parts.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The command module held a cabin for three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The service module provided the command module with propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had two stages: a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"The design allowed the spacecraft to be launched by a single Saturn V rocket, then under development; Apollo relied on Gemini techniques and on semiconductor advances such as MOSFETs and silicon ICs.\"}],\"entities\":[\"NASA\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"MOSFETs\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, abruptly halting Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, all was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"command module\",\"service module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Earth\",\"Moon\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Texas\",\"Atlantic\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"MOSFETs\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"4a9a539e312cd250e218ccc985492005ae19739c6cdb7ffb7bb6cec38d74fad9"}}

